### Justification
The fact-check finds this claim to be **refuted**. Joe Biden explicitly and repeatedly stated during the 2020 presidential campaign, "I am not banning fracking," as documented by [CNN](https://www.cnn.com/2020/08/31/politics/joe-biden-not-banning-fracking/index.html). His running mate Kamala Harris confirmed this position during the vice-presidential debate, and Biden himself said "fracking is not going to be on the chopping block" ([Newsweek](https://www.newsweek.com/biden-says-fracking-jobs-wont-chopping-block-if-hes-elected-1516793)). While Biden proposed ending new oil and gas leasing on federal lands—a policy affecting only a small fraction of overall fracking activity—this is fundamentally different from a nationwide ban on fracking ([Fox Business](https://www.foxbusiness.com/markets/biden-energy-restrictions-jobs-losses-oil-imports-american-petroleum-institute)). As president, Biden has neither signed an executive order nor enacted any federal law banning fracking on private, state, or federal lands ([Newsweek](https://www.newsweek.com/fact-check-does-joe-biden-support-green-new-deal-fracking-ban-1537291)). The historical record of Biden's public statements is consistent: he opposed a fracking ban throughout the campaign and into his presidency.